@brief
Second merge, and this one is not "accept both".

    $ git merge main
    Auto-merging src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
    CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
    CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in src/configuration/errors/default.json

Both branches rewrote the middle of `cancel`. `main` added an audit call
after the refund. Your branch made cancel idempotent, so a second cancel
returns the order instead of refunding it again. The two changes are
compatible; git just cannot see that, because they overlap line for line.

The merged `cancel` keeps the shipped guard, then your idempotency guard,
then the refund, then `main`'s audit call — in that order. `main`'s
constructor change came through cleanly, so `auditService` is already
injected; if the audit line does not survive, nothing uses it and the
build goes red.

The JSON conflict is the easy kind. Both codes are wanted, `main`'s
first, and there is no blank line to preserve.

The resolver is open for reference and does not change.

@keys
| `ma` | drop mark a on this line |
| `"ayy` | yank this line into register a |
| `"ap` | put register a back |
| `:'a,/^=/d` | delete from mark a down to the `=======` line |
| `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` | delete every marker line |

@hints
You are keeping almost all of one side and exactly one line of the other.
Decide which side that is before you delete anything: it is the side with
more of the merged result in it.
--
The one line you need from `main` is the audit call. Get it out of the
way first — yank it into a named register — and then the whole HEAD block
becomes something you can delete in one command.
--
`/^<<<` `Enter` `ma` marks the top of the mess. `/auditService` `Enter`
`"ayy` saves the line. `:'a,/^=/d` removes the marker, the HEAD block and
the separator together. `/^>>>` `Enter` `dd` takes the last marker, `k`
puts you on the refund line and `"ap` drops the audit call back after it.

@solution
`/^<<<` `Enter` `ma` `/auditService` `Enter` `"ayy` `:'a,/^=/d` `Enter`
`/^>>>` `Enter` `dd` `k` `"ap`, then `:bn` `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` `:wqa`.
74 keystrokes.

Three things in here are worth taking away.

**Rescue the minority side first.** When one side is nearly the answer
and the other contributes a line or two, pull those lines into a named
register before you start deleting. After that the conflict is a
delete-everything-between-the-markers job, which is one command. Trying
to delete around the line you want to keep is where people end up with
six `dd`s and a mis-count.

**Use a named register, not the unnamed one.** `"ayy` survives every `dd`
that follows. A plain `yy` would be gone the moment you delete the first
line, and you would not notice until `p` put the wrong thing back.

**A mark is cheaper than a count.** `ma` on the `<<<<<<<` line, then
`:'a,/^=/d` reads as "from where the conflict started to the separator"
regardless of how many lines are in between. Neither number appears in
the command, so neither number can be wrong.

The JSON is a reminder from the last merge: when both sides are adding
lines to a list and no spacing has to be preserved,
`:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` removes all three markers in one go and leaves the two
new lines in the order git wrote them, which is `main`'s first.

Last thing, and it is not a keystroke: read the merged method top to
bottom before you save. Both sides compiling is not the same as the
result being right. The order of the idempotency guard and the refund is
the entire point of your branch, and a merge that puts the refund first
passes review and quietly refunds twice.

